                    <physDesc>
                        <bindingDesc>
                            <binding>
                                <p>A binding leaf is part of a s. xv will containing bequests to
                                    individuals at Beverley and to the prior and monks of the
                                    Carthusian house at London and Hull. Another leaf is part of a
                                    receipt roll of a church at Beverley (s. xv) and a third is from
                                    the inventory of William Burgenoun of Tempsford.</p>
                            </binding>
                        </bindingDesc>
                    </physDesc>
                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1400" notAfter="1499">s.
                            xv</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_567">Warden,
                            Bedfordshire, Cistercian abbey of St Mary the Virgin</orgName> <p>This
                            rejected attribution first appeared in the Supplement to Medieval
                            Libraries of Great Britain (1987).</p><p> The corresponding Medieval
                            Libraries of Great Britain index card was originally written by Ker. He
                            recorded the following quotation: <q>[...] artem moriendi | [..]s
                            constat Willelmo | [... do]mpni Iohannes | [.....]s de Wardon</q> on a
                            torn flyleaf (fol. 2).</p><p> Ker also added the following notes: John
                            [...] was abbot of Warden 1428. John Fraunceys was abbot 1447 and 1454
                            (<title>VCH</title>).</p> <p>Presumably it was when Andrew Watson was
                            preparing the Supplement that he added the word <q>reject</q> to the
                            card, along with the following note: <q>No certainty that it was at
                            Warden</q>. </p></provenance>
                    </history>
